<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Wekesa solved <m>{6\sin^2 x + \cos x - 5 = 0}</m> for <m>0^\circ \le x \le 360^\circ</m>. His working has one mistake.</p><tabular style="border-collapse:collapse;"><row><cell style="padding:3px 12px;">Step 1</cell><cell><m>{6(1 - \cos^2 x) + \cos x - 5 = 0}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:3px 12px;">Step 2</cell><cell><m>{6\cos^2 x - \cos x - 1 = 0}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:3px 12px;">Step 3</cell><cell><m>{(2\cos x - 1)(3\cos x + 1) = 0}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:3px 12px;">Step 4</cell><cell><m>\cos x = {\frac{1}{2}}</m> or <m>\cos x = {-\frac{1}{3}}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:3px 12px;">Step 5</cell><cell><m>x \in {\{60^\circ, 109.5^\circ\}}</m></cell></row></tabular><p>(a) Which step has the mistake?</p><div>[ Step 1 | Step 2 | Step 3 | Step 4 | Step 5 ]  </div><p>(b) Write the correct set of solutions. Give angles that are not whole numbers to 1 decimal place.</p><div><m>x \in</m> <fillin characters="20" name="ans2"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Check each line against the one before.</p><p>Multiplying out step 1 and collecting terms gives <m>{6}\cos^2 x{ - \cos x}{ - 1} = 0</m>, which is step 2. Steps 3 and 4 follow.</p><p>The correct values are <m>\cos x = {\frac{1}{2}}</m> or <m>\cos x = {-\frac{1}{3}}</m>. For each value take the calculator angle <m>\alpha</m> and <m>360^\circ - \alpha</m> (<m>\cos x = 1</m> gives <m>0^\circ</m> and <m>360^\circ</m>; <m>\cos x = -1</m> gives only <m>180^\circ</m>).</p><p>In step 5 Wekesa wrote only the calculator angles and missed the second angle for each value.</p><p>The mistake is in step <m>{5}</m>. The solutions are <m>x \in {\{60^\circ, 109.5^\circ, 250.5^\circ, 300^\circ\}}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p>Step 5</p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\left \{60.0 , 109.5 , 250.5 , 300.0 \right \}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>